60,3,1,talk,Yumi,,I love the idea of supporting the partner groups! Candidate C feels like the safest bet since they already run their own sessions.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Yumi, C is the partner model, but B is the digitisation ghost. We tried that six years ago and left half the images unindexed.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Shion,,"@Benjamin, B is a trap. The digitisation batch failed because we lacked the specific technical skills to index it. We cannot repeat that error.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Minako,,"@Benjamin, B is a capital draw on the endowment. That is a one-time hit, not a recurring trap. The funding structure is actually cleaner there.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"@Yumi, C relies on partner groups, but the bus cuts mean residents in the outer wards cannot reach the main branch.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Daisuke, that is a real worry. But the community halls kept their own timetables despite the road closures! I think C can adapt to local conditions better than a fixed vehicle.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Daisuke, the attendance sheets prove the outer wards are hungry. D brings the service to them, bypassing the bus cuts entirely.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Benjamin, D is a logistical nightmare. The vehicle line already overran by a third. We cannot afford to expand that failure.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Shion, the vehicle overrun is a recurring trap. D locks us into that cost forever. B is a one-time capital draw, which is far safer for the long term.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@Benjamin, D does reach them, but the vehicle costs are a recurring trap. I worry about the long-term burden.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Yumi,,"@Shion, I hear your worry about the vehicle costs. But C uses the partner groups who already have the skills! We just provide the room, avoiding the staffing gap entirely.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Shion, the partner groups proved they can self-manage. C avoids the supervision gap you identified. We just provide the space, not the staff.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Yumi, C is the only option that doesn't require new skills. The partner groups already have the facilitators. We just need to stop trying to supervise them.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Minako,,"@Shion, C relies on partner groups, but the grant only covers twelve months. After that, the flat budget absorbs the recurring costs. That is the structural weakness.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,"@Minako, the funding gap is real. But the evening data shows people are already there, just after we lock up.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Yumi,,"@Minako, that is a valid concern. But the partner groups are already self-sustaining! We just need to trust them to keep going. It feels much more resilient than relying on our fragile vehicle budget.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Minako, the partner groups have already survived six years without our money. C is the only option with a proven track record of not failing.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Shion,,"@Minako, the funding gap is real, but C is the only option that doesn't require new skills. The partner groups already have the facilitators. We just need to stop trying to supervise them.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Minako,,"@Benjamin, their survival is not a business model. The grant expires, and the flat budget absorbs the recurring costs. That is the structural failure I see.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,"@Minako, I understand the risk. But if we do nothing, the outer wards stay locked out. The bus cuts make that impossible to ignore.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
